  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Direct L1 oral-bioavailability outcomes for anthranilic acid NSAIDs and related acids are high: meclofenamate >85% (C06), diflunisal 89.5% (C10), flufenamic acid good (C02), telmisartan 42-58% (C01, C03), and 2,4-D rapid/complete (C11), supporting F>=20% for the query's acidic anthranilic scaffold.",
      "card_ids": ["C06","C10","C02","C01","C03","C11"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Sulfonamide-containing drugs also meet the F>=20% threshold: celecoxib 20-40% (C07), pazopanib 21.4% (C13), and BMS-275183 24% (C22), showing that the sulfamoyl group is not an affirmative low-F liability.",
      "card_ids": ["C07","C13","C22"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 solubility/absorption records for ionized acids and sulfonamide drugs are consistent with meaningful absorption: diclofenac sodium solubility 2.43 g/L and intestinal permeability 10e-6 cm/s (C131), tamsulosin >90% absorbed (C147) and 100% absorbed (C153), and chlorothiazide solubility 3.1 mg/mL at pH 8 (C165).",
      "card_ids": ["C131","C147","C153","C165"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic-clearance records for structurally related loop diuretics indicate low hepatic first-pass loss: bumetanide is a low-hepatic-extraction drug with almost negligible direct hepatic extraction (C232), azosemide has low rat hepatic microsomal intrinsic clearance (C239, C244), and tamsulosin shows no significant hepatic first-pass difference (C274). These support that first-pass metabolism is not an affirmative barrier to F>=20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C232","C239","C244","C274"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The many L6 diclofenac CYP2C9 records are not applicable to furosemide and provide no negative F evidence; they do not overturn the high prediction.",
      "card_ids": ["C230","C237","C251"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The low physicochemical prior is an uncertain prior, not affirmative low-F evidence; low in vitro permeability can coexist with high oral F in this class (C10, C06, C02).",
      "card_ids": ["C10","C06","C02"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C232","C239","C244","C274"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No furosemide-specific hepatic clearance or metabolic stability record was supplied; L6 records are for other loop diuretics and drugs.",
    "No direct furosemide absolute F, AUC, Cmax, permeability, solubility, or fraction-absorbed record was supplied.",
    "No cystic fibrosis-specific pharmacokinetic data were supplied; extrapolation from general human and animal populations is indirect.",
    "Species differences (rat vs human) in intrinsic clearance for the loop-diuretic analogs are not resolved.",
    "First-pass hepatic metabolism and gut-wall extraction for furosemide are not directly measured."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains high (F>=20%). L1 direct oral-bioavailability outcomes for anthranilic acid NSAIDs and related acids are high, and sulfonamide-containing drugs meet the threshold. L6 hepatic-clearance records for structurally related loop diuretics (bumetanide, azosemide, tamsulosin) indicate low hepatic first-pass loss, supporting rather than undermining the high prediction. The low physicochemical prior is an uncertain prior, not affirmative low-F evidence. Confidence remains moderate because no furosemide-specific F or clearance record was supplied."
}

